{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Oh that Ishmael might live before You</b>. The meaning of this word (לוּ) everywhere is like the sense of \"if\" (אִם), and it is composed with the word \"were it not\" (לוּלֵי) (Genesis 31:42), and its sense is \"if not\" (אִם לֹא), and it is sometimes written with an aleph — אִלּוּ (Genesis 43:10). And likewise they further combine it and say אִלּוּ, as in \"and if indeed for bondmen and for handmaids\" (Esther 7:4), \"and if he live a thousand years twice\" (Ecclesiastes 6:6); and the meaning of the word is \"if only\" (אִם לוּ), and its sense is like \"if if\" (אִם אִם), and the doubling comes for reinforcement, like \"Has it been only with Moses\" (הֲרַק אַךְ) (Numbers 12:2), \"Is it because there are no graves\" (הַמִבְּלִי אֵין) (Exodus 14:11), and their similars. And Abraham said: If Ishmael will live before You, I will desire this blessing that You have blessed me with through Sarah's seed, for at first He had promised him \"he who shall come forth from your own bowels shall be your heir\" (Genesis 15:4), and behold the one who inherits him is unique, and he thought it was Ishmael, and now that it was said to him that Sarah would bear, he understood that he is the heir, and feared lest Ishmael die; therefore he said this. <b>May he live before You</b>. Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 17:18) explained: may he live in Your fear, like \"walk before Me\" (Genesis 17:1) — serve before Me. And this is not correct, because He said \"And as for Ishmael, I have heard you\"; but its meaning is: may he live and may his seed endure forever."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:30:24.993897625Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}